{"name":"Alien","hex":"#415764","fetched":"2026-10-20","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Alien","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative form of Alian."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Alien"}],"entries":[{"title":"Extraterrestrial life","blocks":[{"text":"Extraterrestrial life, or alien life, is life that originates from another world rather than on Earth. No extraterrestrial life has yet been detected. Such life might range from simple forms such as microbes to intelligent beings, possibly bringing forth civilizations that might be far more, or far less, advanced than humans. The Drake equation speculates about the existence of sapient life elsewhere in the universe. The science of extraterrestrial life is known as astrobiology."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extraterrestrial_life","wikidata":"Q181508"},{"title":"Extraterrestrial intelligence","blocks":[{"text":"Extraterrestrial intelligence (ETI) refers to hypothetical beings – extraterrestrial life or more artificial beings – having the intelligence to perform similar cognitive abilities as humans. No such life has ever been verifiably observed to exist. The question of whether other inhabited worlds might exist has been debated since ancient history. The modern form of the concept emerged when the Copernican Revolution demonstrated that the Earth was a planet revolving around the Sun, and other planets were, conversely, other worlds. The question of whether other inhabited planets or moons exist was a natural consequence of this new understanding. It has become one of the most speculative questions in science and is a central theme of both science fiction and popular culture."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extraterrestrial_intelligence","wikidata":"Q15107669"},{"title":"Alien (franchise)","blocks":[{"text":"Alien is an American science fiction horror and action media franchise created by screenwriters Dan O'Bannon and Ronald Shusett, which began as the 1979 film Alien. The series primarily follows deadly encounters between humans and the extraterrestrial Xenomorphs in the future."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_(franchise)","wikidata":"Q1990792"},{"title":"Alien (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Alien is a 1979 science fiction horror film directed by Ridley Scott and written by Dan O'Bannon, based on a story by O'Bannon and Ronald Shusett. It follows a commercial starship crew who investigate a derelict space vessel and are hunted by a deadly extraterrestrial creature. The film stars Tom Skerritt, Sigourney Weaver, Veronica Cartwright, Harry Dean Stanton, John Hurt, Ian Holm, and Yaphet Kotto. It was produced by Gordon Carroll, David Giler, and Walter Hill through their company Brandywine Productions and was distributed by 20th Century-Fox. Giler and Hill revised and made additions to the script; Shusett was the executive producer. The alien creatures and environments were designed by the Swiss artist H. R. Giger, while the concept artists Ron Cobb and Chris Foss designed the other sets."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c3/Alien_movie_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_(film)","wikidata":"Q103569"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/MarsCuriosityRover-Drilling-Sol170%2B%2B-2.jpg/500px-MarsCuriosityRover-Drilling-Sol170%2B%2B-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/MarsCuriosityRover-Drilling-Sol170%2B%2B-2.jpg/1280px-MarsCuriosityRover-Drilling-Sol170%2B%2B-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/MarsCuriosityRover-Drilling-Sol170%2B%2B-2.jpg/500px-MarsCuriosityRover-Drilling-Sol170%2B%2B-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMarsCuriosityRover-Drilling-Sol170%2B%2B-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Habitable_Worlds_2.jpg/500px-Habitable_Worlds_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Habitable_Worlds_2.jpg/500px-Habitable_Worlds_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Besides Earth, Mars, Europa and Enceladus are the most likely places in the Solar System to find life.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHabitable_Worlds_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/NASA-WhatBiosignaturesDoesLifeProduce-20180625.jpg/330px-NASA-WhatBiosignaturesDoesLifeProduce-20180625.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/NASA-WhatBiosignaturesDoesLifeProduce-20180625.jpg/330px-NASA-WhatBiosignaturesDoesLifeProduce-20180625.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lifeforms produce a variety of biosignatures that may be detectable by telescopes.[78][79]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANASA-WhatBiosignaturesDoesLifeProduce-20180625.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Green_Bank_100m_diameter_Radio_Telescope.jpg/500px-Green_Bank_100m_diameter_Radio_Telescope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Green_Bank_100m_diameter_Radio_Telescope.jpg/500px-Green_Bank_100m_diameter_Radio_Telescope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Green Bank Telescope is one of the radio telescopes used by the Breakthrough Listen project to search for alien communications.","link":"https://e